    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Thanks. Can you go into more detail about the ventilation aspect? Specifically, how do those racking configurations influence the way gases might accumulate in \"dead zones\" or corners of the warehouse? I'm trying to understand if certain areas might have much higher ignition risks than others due to poor airflow.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The way gases accumulate is directly tied to the geometry of the racking system, which can create pockets of stagnant air, or \"dead zones,\" where flammable vapors can concentrate to explosive levels. Deep, narrow aisles, especially when filled with tightly packed, solvent-laden pallets, act like funnels that can trap heavier-than-air vapors near the floor. These low-lying zones are prime spots for ignition, as the vapors pool there, waiting for a spark, much like a poorly managed, low-level storage area in a cavernous, poorly ventilated section of the warehouse. Conversely, high-density setups might also trap lighter vapors near the ceiling if the ventilation is inadequate.",
